\begin{abstract}
Every primitive Pythagorean triple $a^2+b^2=c^2$ is an instance of the $abc$ situation $A+B=C$ with $A=a^2$, $B=b^2$, $C=c^2$, and the primitive triples form a ternary tree, rooted at $(3,4,5)$, generated by three unimodular matrices. We develop the complete quality theory of this tree. The starting point is the \emph{radical collapse} $\rad(a^2b^2c^2)=\rad(abc)$, which reduces the $abc$ quality of a node to $q=2\log c/\log\rad(abc)$ and converts every quality threshold into an exact integer inequality: $q>1$ if and only if $\rad(abc)<c^2$, $q<2$ if and only if $\rad(abc)>c$, and more generally $q>m/k$ if and only if $\rad(abc)^m<c^{2k}$. Using this dictionary we prove: an unconditional lower edge $q>2/3$ for every node, in the quantitative form $q\ge 2\log c/(3\log c-\log 2)\ge \tfrac23+2\log 2/(9\log c)$; an unconditional gap $q<2$ for every node whose product is not powerful, together with the observation that the residual gap is only $\Theta(1/\log c)$, so that a \emph{uniform} bound $q\le 2-\varepsilon$ is genuinely of $abc$-strength; conditional sharp bounds $q\le13/10$ (under an effective $abc$ inequality) and $q\le1+2\varepsilon$ (under the Masser--Oesterl\'e conjecture); an explicit infinite family of $abc$ hits, namely the almost-isosceles nodes with parameter $n=d^{2^k}-1$ for odd $d\ge3$ with $d\le 2^k$, which is doubly exponentially sparse; and a universal depth law: every step multiplies the hypotenuse by at most the squared silver ratio $3+2\sqrt2=(1+\sqrt2)^2$, so a node at depth $n$ obeys $c\le 5(3+2\sqrt2)^n$ and the depth-$n$ quality window is $2/3<q\le 2(\log5+n\log(3+2\sqrt2))/\log\rad(abc)$. Consequently the entire depth dependence of the spectrum is carried by the radical alone: the tree isolates in exact form the geometric half of the $abc$ problem and leaves the multiplicative half standing alone. Explicit data --- including $q(3,4,5)\approx0.9464$, $q(7,24,25)\approx1.2040$, non-monotonicity of quality under descent, and the bracket $5/4<q(36207,18424,40625)<4/3$ for the best node among all $159\,139$ nodes with hypotenuse below $10^6$ --- show that the tree's high-quality region, while infinite, stays well below the $abc$ record quality $\approx1.63$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} $abc$ conjecture, quality, radical, Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, silver ratio, lifting the exponent, Pell numbers.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The $abc$ conjecture and quality}

For a positive integer $n$ write $\rad(n)=\prod_{p\mid n}p$ for its \emph{radical}, the product of the distinct primes dividing $n$ (with $\rad(1)=1$). An \emph{$abc$ triple} is a triple of positive integers with
\[
A+B=C,\qquad \gcd(A,B)=1 .
\]
The Masser--Oesterl\'e conjecture (1985) asserts that for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $K_\varepsilon$ with $C\le K_\varepsilon\rad(ABC)^{1+\varepsilon}$ for all such triples. The standard scalar attached to a single triple is its \emph{quality}
\[
q(A,B,C)=\frac{\log C}{\log \rad(ABC)} .
\]
A triple with $q>1$ is an \emph{$abc$ hit}. The conjecture is equivalent to: for each $\varepsilon>0$ only finitely many triples have $q>1+\varepsilon$. The largest known quality is $q\approx1.6299$, from $2+3^{10}\cdot109=23^5$; no triple with $q\ge2$ is known, and even the ceiling $q<2$ is not known unconditionally.

A persistent obstacle to intuition is that no natural infinite family of $abc$ triples has a computable quality structure.

\subsection{Pythagorean triples as $abc$ triples}

Let $a,b,c$ be positive integers with $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $\gcd(a,b)=1$. Setting $A=a^2$, $B=b^2$, $C=c^2$ produces an $abc$ triple, with quality $q=\log(c^2)/\log\rad(a^2b^2c^2)$.

Primitive Pythagorean triples have an exceptionally rigid global structure. Berggren (1934), rediscovered by Barning (1963) and Hall (1970), showed that the set of primitive triples with $a$ odd is the vertex set of an infinite rooted ternary tree: the root is $(3,4,5)$, and the three children of a node are obtained by
\[
M_A=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2&2\\2&-1&2\\2&-2&3\end{pmatrix},\quad
M_B=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&2\\2&1&2\\2&2&3\end{pmatrix},\quad
M_C=\begin{pmatrix}-1&2&2\\-2&1&2\\-2&2&3\end{pmatrix}
\]
acting on the column vector $(a,b,c)^{\mathsf T}$. Each primitive triple occurs exactly once, at the end of a unique finite word in $\{A,B,C\}$, and inverting the matrices returns any triple to $(3,4,5)$ in finitely many steps. This is a fully explicit infinite family of $abc$ triples with explicit generation, descent, and growth constant. We determine what can, and cannot, be said exactly about its quality spectrum.

\section{Radical toolkit}

\begin{lemma}[basic bounds]\label{lem:basic}
For $n\ge1$ we have $\rad(n)\mid n$, hence $\rad(n)\le n$. If $n\ge2$ then $\rad(n)\ge2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The radical is a product of pairwise coprime prime divisors of $n$, so it divides $n$; with $n>0$ this gives $\rad(n)\le n$. If $n\ge2$, some prime $p$ divides $n$, hence divides $\rad(n)$, so $\rad(n)\ge p\ge2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[submultiplicativity]\label{lem:submul}
For $m,n\ge1$, $\rad(mn)\le\rad(m)\rad(n)$, with equality when $\gcd(m,n)=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The primes of $mn$ form the union of those of $m$ and of $n$, so
$\rad(mn)\prod_{p\mid m,\,p\mid n}p=\rad(m)\rad(n)$,
and the second factor on the left is at least $1$; it equals $1$ exactly when the prime sets are disjoint.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[exponent loss]\label{lem:lte}
If $p$ is prime and $t\ge1$ then $\rad(p^t)=p$. If moreover $p^s\mid n$ with $s\ge1$ and $n\ge1$, then $\rad(n)\,p^{\,s-1}\le n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The first claim is immediate. For the second write $t=v_p(n)\ge s$ and $n=p^tu$ with $p\nmid u$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:submul} in the coprime case, $\rad(n)=p\,\rad(u)$, hence
$\rad(n)p^{s-1}=p^{s}\rad(u)\le p^{s}u\le p^{t}u=n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[powers are free]\label{lem:powfree}
For $m\ge1$, $\rad(n^m)=\rad(n)$; and for positive $a,b,c$ and exponents $r,s,t\ge1$, $\rad(a^rb^sc^t)=\rad(abc)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Raising to a power changes no prime's presence, only its multiplicity, and the radical depends only on the set of primes present.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:lte} is the engine behind every $abc$ hit constructed below.

\section{Quality and the exact dictionary}

\begin{definition}
For positive integers $a,b,c$ with $a^2+b^2=c^2$, the \emph{quality} is
$q(a,b,c)=\log(c^2)/\log\rad(a^2b^2c^2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[radical collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
For positive $a,b,c$ we have $\rad(a^2b^2c^2)=\rad(abc)$, hence
\[
q(a,b,c)=\frac{2\log c}{\log \rad(abc)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:powfree} with $r=s=t=2$, together with $\log(c^2)=2\log c$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} is what makes the tree tractable: the squares, the source of most of the difficulty in a general $abc$ instance, disappear from the radical, and the whole arithmetic content is carried by the single integer $abc$.

\begin{theorem}[threshold dictionary]\label{thm:dict}
Let $a,b\ge1$, $c\ge2$, $abc\ge2$, and let $m,k$ be positive integers. Then
\[
q(a,b,c)>\frac mk \iff \rad(abc)^{m}<c^{2k},\qquad
q(a,b,c)<\frac mk \iff c^{2k}<\rad(abc)^{m}.
\]
In particular $q>1\iff\rad(abc)<c^2$ and $q<2\iff c<\rad(abc)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}, $\log\rad(abc)>0$; clearing denominators, $2\log c/\log\rad(abc)>m/k$ is equivalent to $\log(c^{2k})>\log(\rad(abc)^m)$, hence to $\rad(abc)^m<c^{2k}$ by strict monotonicity of the logarithm.
\end{proof}

Thus every question about the quality spectrum is, verbatim, a question about the size of one radical relative to a power of the hypotenuse; no analytic residue remains.

\section{Structure of the tree}

\begin{definition}
A triple $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers is a \emph{tree node} if it is obtained from $(3,4,5)$ by a finite (possibly empty) sequence of the maps $M_A,M_B,M_C$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[tree invariant]\label{thm:inv}
Every tree node $(a,b,c)$ satisfies $a\ge3$, $b\ge3$, $a<c$, $b<c$, $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $\gcd(a,b)=1$; in particular $c\ge5$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on the length of the generating word. The root satisfies all six conditions. Each matrix preserves the Pythagorean relation, by a polynomial identity; for $M_B$,
\[
(a+2b+2c)^2+(2a+b+2c)^2-(2a+2b+3c)^2=a^2+b^2-c^2,
\]
and similarly for $M_A$ and $M_C$. Primitivity is preserved because the matrices are unimodular with integral inverses, so a common prime factor of the new legs descends to one of the old legs. The size inequalities are linear arithmetic; finally $c^2=a^2+b^2\ge18$ and $c>a\ge3$ force $c\ge5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every tree node is an $abc$ triple: $a^2+b^2=c^2$ with $\gcd(a^2,b^2)=1$ and $\rad(a^2b^2c^2)=\rad(abc)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:elem}
If $a,b\ge3$ and $a^2+b^2=c^2$ then $2ab\le c^2$ and $2c\le ab$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The first is $(a-b)^2\ge0$. For the second,
$(ab)^2-(2c)^2=a^2b^2-4a^2-4b^2=(a^2-4)(b^2-4)-16\ge25-16=9>0$.
\end{proof}

\section{The lower edge of the spectrum}

\begin{theorem}[quantitative lower edge]\label{thm:lower}
Let $a,b\ge3$, $c\ge5$ with $a^2+b^2=c^2$. Then
\[
q(a,b,c)\ \ge\ \frac{2\log c}{3\log c-\log 2}\ >\ \frac23 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:elem}, $2ab\le c^2$, so $abc\le c^3/2$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}, $\rad(abc)\le abc\le c^3/2$. Taking logarithms, $0<\log\rad(abc)\le3\log c-\log2$; dividing the positive numerator $2\log c$ by a smaller positive denominator gives the first inequality. The second is $6\log c>6\log c-2\log2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[rate]\label{cor:rate}
Every tree node satisfies $q(a,b,c)\ge\tfrac23+\dfrac{2\log2}{9\log c}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
With $L=\log c\ge\log5$ and $D=3L-\log2>0$,
\[
\frac{2L}{D}-\frac23-\frac{2\log2}{9L}
=\frac{18L^2-6LD-2D\log2}{9LD}
=\frac{2(\log2)^2}{9LD}>0 .\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

So $2/3$ is a genuine barrier that can only be approached along nodes with $c\to\infty$, and then no faster than $1/\log c$. Numerically, the smallest quality among nodes with $c\le3\cdot10^5$ is $0.6920$, against the bound $0.6785$ at that size.

\begin{remark}
The bound is saturated in shape by squarefree products: if $abc$ is squarefree then $\rad(abc)=abc\asymp c^3$ and $q\approx2/3$. The lower edge of the spectrum is exactly the squarefree regime, where the $abc$ mechanism has nothing to exploit.
\end{remark}

\section{The upper edge}

\begin{theorem}[unconditional gap off the powerful locus]\label{thm:upper}
Let $a,b\ge3$, $c\ge5$, $a^2+b^2=c^2$, and suppose $abc\le\rad(abc)^2$ (which holds, for instance, whenever $abc$ is squarefree). Then $q(a,b,c)<2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:elem}, $2c\le ab$, hence $2c^2\le abc\le\rad(abc)^2$, so $c<\rad(abc)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dict}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[the gap is not uniform]\label{rem:notuniform}
Theorem~\ref{thm:upper} yields
\[
2-q\ \ge\ \frac{2\log(\rad(abc)/c)}{\log\rad(abc)},
\]
which in the worst case is only $\Theta(1/\log c)$. Hence no uniform bound $q\le2-\varepsilon$ over the tree follows from this mechanism, and this is not a defect of the argument: a uniform gap asserts $\rad(abc)\ge c^{1+\delta}$ for all nodes, a power-saving lower bound on radicals of $abc$-strength.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[tree $abc$ theorem, effective form]\label{thm:eff}
Suppose $K$ is a constant for which $C^{12}\le K\rad(ABC)^{13}$ holds for all coprime $A+B=C$ in positive integers. Then every primitive Pythagorean triple with $c\ge2$ and $K\le c^4$ satisfies $q(a,b,c)\le 13/10$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the hypothesis to $A=a^2,B=b^2,C=c^2$, writing $R=\rad(abc)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}:
$c^{24}=(c^2)^{12}\le KR^{13}\le c^4R^{13}$, so $c^{20}\le R^{13}$, i.e. $20\log c\le13\log R$. Hence $q=2\log c/\log R\le 26/20=13/10$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[conditional asymptotic law]\label{thm:asym}
Assume the Masser--Oesterl\'e conjecture. For every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $C_0=C_0(\varepsilon)$ such that every primitive Pythagorean triple with $c\ge C_0$ satisfies $q(a,b,c)\le1+2\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $K=K_\varepsilon$ and apply the conjecture to $A=a^2,B=b^2,C=c^2$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}, $c^2\le KR^{1+\varepsilon}$ with $R=\rad(abc)$, i.e.
$2\log c\le\log K+(1+\varepsilon)\log R$.
Put $M=\max(\log K,0)$ and $C_0=\exp\bigl(\tfrac M2(1+\tfrac{1+\varepsilon}{\varepsilon})\bigr)$. For $c\ge C_0$ one deduces $\log K\le\varepsilon\log R$: trivially if $\log K\le0$, and otherwise from $\log R\ge(2\log c-\log K)/(1+\varepsilon)$ together with the lower bound on $\log c$. Substituting gives $2\log c\le(1+2\varepsilon)\log R$, i.e. $q\le1+2\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:lower} and~\ref{thm:asym}: under $abc$ the limiting quality spectrum of the tree lies in $(2/3,1]$.

\section{Infinitely many hits, and their sparsity}

\subsection{The almost-isosceles spine}

Iterating $M_A$ from the root gives the \emph{$A$-spine}
\[
T_n=(2n+1,\;2n(n+1),\;2n^2+2n+1),\qquad n\ge1,
\]
so $T_1=(3,4,5)$, $T_2=(5,12,13)$, $T_3=(7,24,25)$, \dots, the triples with $c=b+1$; $T_n$ sits at the end of the word $A^{\,n-1}$. Submultiplicativity gives
\[
\rad(abc)\le(2n+1)\cdot2\rad(n)\rad(n+1)\cdot c .
\]

\begin{proposition}[hit criterion]\label{prop:crit}
If $n\ge3$ and $(2n+1)\cdot2\rad(n)\rad(n+1)<2n^2+2n+1$, then $q(T_n)>1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Multiply the hypothesis by $c$ to get $\rad(abc)<c^2$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dict}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The family $n=d^{2^k}-1$}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:two}
Let $d\ge3$ be odd and $k\ge1$. Then $2^{k+2}$ divides $d^{2^k}-1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Factor $d^{2^k}-1=(d-1)(d+1)(d^2+1)(d^4+1)\cdots(d^{2^{k-1}}+1)$. Since $d$ is odd, $(d-1)(d+1)$ is a product of consecutive even numbers, hence divisible by $8$, and each of the remaining $k-1$ factors is even.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[explicit infinite hit family]\label{thm:hits}
Let $d\ge3$ be odd, let $k\ge1$ satisfy $d\le2^k$, and put $n=d^{2^k}-1$. Then $T_n$ is a tree node with $q(T_n)>1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two radical collapses. First, $n+1=d^{2^k}$ is a pure power, so $\rad(n+1)=\rad(d)\le d$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:powfree}. Second, $2^{k+2}\mid n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:two}, so $\rad(n)\le n/2^{k+1}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:lte}. Hence
\[
\rad(abc)\le(2n+1)\cdot2\cdot\frac{n}{2^{k+1}}\cdot d\cdot c=\frac{(2n+1)nd}{2^{k}}\,c ,
\]
and since $d\le2^k$ we get $(2n+1)nd/2^k\le2n^2+n<2n^2+2n+1=c$. Therefore $\rad(abc)<c^2$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:dict} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every bound $N$ there is a tree node with $c>N$ and $q>1$; indeed such a family exists for every odd base $d\ge3$.
\end{corollary}

The condition $d\le2^k$ is exactly the balance point of the two collapses.

\begin{theorem}[double-exponential sparsity]\label{thm:sparse}
Let $n_k=3^{2^k}-1$ and $c_k=2n_k^2+2n_k+1$. Then $c_{k+1}<c_k^2<4c_{k+1}$ for all $k\ge0$; consequently the number of members of the family with hypotenuse below $X$ is $O(\log\log X)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $m=3^{2^k}$, so $c_k=2m^2-2m+1$ and $c_{k+1}=2m^4-2m^2+1$. Then
\[
c_k^2-c_{k+1}=2m^4-8m^3+10m^2-4m=2m(m-1)^2(m-2)>0
\]
for $m\ge3$, and $4c_{k+1}-c_k^2=4m^4+8m^3-16m^2+4m+3>0$ for $m\ge1$. Iterating $c_{k+1}\asymp c_k^2$ gives $\log\log c_k\asymp k$.
\end{proof}

Thus the only mechanism producing provable hits here is doubly exponentially sparse, consistent with the observed rarity of hits ($0.43\%$ of nodes with $c\le3\cdot10^5$).

\section{The silver-ratio depth law}

Let $\sigma=1+\sqrt2$ be the silver ratio, so $\sigma^2=3+2\sqrt2\approx5.828427$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sqrt2}
Any Pythagorean triple satisfies $a+b\le\sqrt2\,c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$(a+b)^2=c^2+2ab\le2c^2$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:elem}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[one step multiplies the hypotenuse by at most $\sigma^2$]\label{thm:step}
If $(a,b,c)$ is a tree node and $(a',b',c')$ one of its children, then $c'\le(3+2\sqrt2)c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The three matrices give $c'\in\{2a+2b+3c,\;2a-2b+3c,\;-2a+2b+3c\}$, and in every case $c'\le2(a+b)+3c\le(3+2\sqrt2)c$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:sqrt2}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:depth}
A node reached by a word of length $n$ satisfies $c\le5(3+2\sqrt2)^n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[two-sided law on the $B$-spine]\label{prop:pell}
Along the all-$B$ branch the hypotenuses satisfy $c_{n+1}=6c_n-c_{n-1}$ with $c_0=5$, $c_1=29$, giving $5,29,169,985,5741,33461,195025,\dots$, and
\[
5^{\,n+1}\le c_n\le5(3+2\sqrt2)^n .
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The upper bound is Corollary~\ref{cor:depth}. For the lower bound, induction: if $c_n\ge5c_{n-1}$ then $c_{n+1}=6c_n-c_{n-1}\ge6c_n-c_n/5\ge5c_n$, and $c_1=29\ge5c_0$.
\end{proof}

Numerically $c_n/(5(3+2\sqrt2)^n)\to0.995$: the squared silver ratio is the exact growth constant of the tree, attained on the $B$-spine.

\begin{theorem}[depth-$n$ quality window]\label{thm:window}
For every word of length $n$ in $\{A,B,C\}$ the node $(a,b,c)$ it reaches satisfies
\[
\log c\le\log5+n\log(3+2\sqrt2),\qquad q(a,b,c)\log\rad(abc)=2\log c,
\]
and hence
\[
\frac23\ <\ q(a,b,c)\ \le\ \frac{2\bigl(\log5+n\log(3+2\sqrt2)\bigr)}{\log\rad(abc)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:depth}, Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} in cleared-denominator form, and Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Interpretation.} In $q=2\log c/\log\rad(abc)$ the numerator is pinned by depth alone, uniformly over all $3^n$ words at depth $n$. All variation in the spectrum comes from the denominator. The tree therefore factors the $abc$ problem into a geometric part, solved exactly by the silver ratio, and a multiplicative part, which is the conjecture.

