\begin{theorem}[non-monotonicity under descent]
$q(5,12,13)<1<q(7,24,25)$ and $q(105,88,137)<1$, where each triple is the parent of the next. Hence quality is not monotone along the tree.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\rad(5\cdot12\cdot13)=390>13^2$; $\rad(7\cdot24\cdot25)=210<25^2$; $\rad(105\cdot88\cdot137)=316470>137^2$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dict}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[bracket for the best node found]
The node $(36207,18424,40625)$, reached by the word $CCCACCBC$, satisfies
\[
\frac54\ <\ q(36207,18424,40625)\ <\ \frac43 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Here $\rad(abc)=19118190$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:dict} the two claims are the finite integer comparisons $19118190^{5}<40625^{8}$ and $40625^{6}<19118190^{4}$, both of which hold.
\end{proof}

An exhaustive scan of all $159\,139$ tree nodes with $c\le10^6$ finds no node of larger quality; the observed spectrum lies in $[0.692,1.2659]$, is unimodal with peak near $q\approx0.73$, and contains hits at density $0.43\%$ in the range $c\le3\cdot10^5$. The second- and third-best nodes, $(239,28560,28561)$ at depth $118$ ($q\approx1.2649$) and $(14553,144896,145625)$ at depth $22$ ($q\approx1.2620$), sit at wildly different depths.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Exact threshold test.} To decide $q(a,b,c)>m/k$: compute $R=\rad(abc)$ by factoring $abc$, then return the verdict of the exact big-integer comparison $R^m<c^{2k}$. One factorization plus $O(\log(mk))$ big-integer multiplications; no floating point, and the answer is certified.

\paragraph{(B) Breadth-first spectrum scan.} Seed a queue with $(3,4,5)$; pop a node, record its quality, and push those of its three images under $M_A,M_B,M_C$ whose hypotenuse does not exceed the cutoff $X$. Correctness follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:inv} and termination from the fact that each matrix strictly increases the hypotenuse. Empirically there are $\approx0.16X$ nodes below $X$.

\paragraph{(C) Certified hits without factoring.} For the family of Theorem~\ref{thm:hits} the hypotenuse cannot be factored. Instead certify from the proof: with $n=d^{2^k}-1$, $a=2n+1$, $c=2n^2+2n+1$,
\[
\rad(abc)\le B(d,k):=a\cdot2\lfloor n/2^{k+1}\rfloor\cdot d\cdot c,
\qquad q\ \ge\ \frac{2\log c}{\log B(d,k)} ,
\]
computable in polylogarithmically many big-integer operations. For $(d,k)=(3,6)$ this certifies $q\ge1.0109$ for a $62$-digit hypotenuse; for $(7,6)$, $q\ge1.0044$ for a $109$-digit one.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the model solves}

The tree delivers in closed form every ingredient of an $abc$ family except one: membership (Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}); the radical (Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}); all thresholds (Theorem~\ref{thm:dict}); growth (Theorem~\ref{thm:window}); the floor $2/3$ with rate (Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}, Corollary~\ref{cor:rate}); and infinitely many hits along an explicit family (Theorem~\ref{thm:hits}, Theorem~\ref{thm:sparse}).

\subsection{What the model does not solve, and why that is informative}

An exactly computed supremum and an exact depth-$n$ distribution law are \emph{not} obtained, and the obstruction is structural.

First, the supremum is $abc$-strength: by Theorem~\ref{thm:dict}, $\sup q<2-\varepsilon$ over the tree asserts $\rad(abc)>c^{2/(2-\varepsilon)}$ for all nodes, a power-saving lower bound on radicals; the unconditional mechanism gives only $\Theta(1/\log c)$ (Remark~\ref{rem:notuniform}), whereas a single effective $abc$ inequality gives $q\le13/10$ at once (Theorem~\ref{thm:eff}).

Second, the distribution is governed by $\rad(n)$, $\rad(n+1)$ and $\rad(2n^2+2n+1)$ along the spine --- precisely the objects controlled only by $abc$ heuristics.

This negative result localizes the difficulty. The geometric side of $abc$ --- generation, growth, descent, radical simplification --- is exactly solvable here; the multiplicative side is untouched, and provably must be. The tree is not a solved instance of $abc$; it is a system in which every degree of freedom except the essential one has been integrated out.

\subsection{Comparison with the general picture}

The record over the tree ($q\approx1.2659$) is far below the global record $q\approx1.6299$: structured families appear to be poor sources of extreme hits. Hits occur at density $\approx0.4\%$ in the scanned range while the provable family has density $O(\log\log X/X)$, mirroring the gap between provable and observed hits in general. Finally, the floor $2/3$ has no analogue for general $abc$ triples, where qualities can be arbitrarily close to $0$; it is a genuine feature of the Pythagorean shape, coming from $2ab\le c^2$.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Sharp lower edge.} Is the constant $2\log2/9$ in Corollary~\ref{cor:rate} optimal? Equivalently, how often is $abc$ squarefree along the tree, and how close to $c^3/2$ can $\rad(abc)$ come? This is a squarefree-values problem for a quaternary form.
\item \textbf{The high-quality region.} Characterize the set of words whose nodes are hits. Hits occur at depths $6,8,22,118,\dots$ with no evident pattern, and non-monotonicity rules out branch-monotone descriptions. Is the language of hit-words non-regular?
\item \textbf{Denser provable hit families.} Theorem~\ref{thm:hits} yields $O(\log\log X)$ hits below $X$. Can a Pythagorean family with $\gg X^{\delta}$ hits be produced, e.g. by exploiting simultaneous power-richness of $n$, $n+1$ and $2n^2+2n+1$?
\item \textbf{Other spines.} The $C$-spine and mixed periodic words give further families with linear-recurrence hypotenuses; a uniform treatment of eventually periodic words would define quality on the boundary of the tree.
\item \textbf{Distribution at depth $n$.} Predict, from standard heuristics for radicals of polynomial values, the limiting distribution of $q$ over the $3^n$ nodes at depth $n$ --- numerically a stable unimodal shape peaked near $0.73$ --- and test against deep scans.
\item \textbf{Beyond squares.} The collapse $\rad(a^rb^sc^t)=\rad(abc)$ holds for all exponents; applying the dictionary to Fermat--Catalan equations $x^p+y^q=z^r$ with $1/p+1/q+1/r<1$ extends the model at the cost of losing the tree.
\item \textbf{Effective constants.} Determine the best explicit ceiling for $\sup q$ obtainable from currently known effective results, which are far weaker than the conjecture.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Reading $a^2+b^2=c^2$ as an instance of $A+B=C$ turns the oldest parametrized family in mathematics into an infinite family of $abc$ triples, and the ternary tree structure turns that family into something rare: an $abc$ family whose geometry is completely explicit. The collapse $\rad(a^2b^2c^2)=\rad(abc)$ makes the quality a ratio of two transparent quantities; the squared silver ratio fixes the numerator as a function of depth; the inequality $2ab\le c^2$ fixes an unconditional floor of $2/3$ with an exact rate; and lifting the exponent produces infinitely many hits along an explicit, doubly exponentially sparse family. What remains --- whether the supremum of the spectrum is $1$, $13/10$, or anything below $2$ --- is exactly the content of the $abc$ conjecture restricted to a family where nothing else is in the way.

